1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,210
Russia's armored force is part museum and
part laboratory. Old hulls are pulled out

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:12,120
of storage, new ones are built in small
numbers, and both meet the same drones.

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:17,657
This is a technical inventory of every
tank and armored vehicle in Russian

4
00:00:17,657 --> 00:00:22,969
service today. Fifty-two machines. Eight
categories. Open sources only.

5
00:00:22,969 --> 00:00:29,875
We begin with the main battle tanks: eight
families, from nineteen-forties steel to

6
00:00:29,875 --> 00:00:31,955
the newest welded turret.

7
00:00:31,955 --> 00:00:38,774
We start where the Soviet school begins.
The T-54 and T-55 are seventy-year-old

8
00:00:38,774 --> 00:00:46,111
designs, pulled out of deep storage by the
thousand. A hundred-millimeter rifled gun,

9
00:00:46,111 --> 00:00:53,189
homogeneous steel armor, and no thermal
sight. But a running tank is still a tank.

10
00:00:53,189 --> 00:00:58,246
The T-62 introduced the smoothbore tank
gun. Its M and MV upgrades bolt on

11
00:00:58,246 --> 00:01:03,986
composite cheeks, a laser rangefinder and
missiles. Obsolete on paper, and common on

12
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:07,881
the battlefield, because Russia has
thousands in reserve.

13
00:01:07,881 --> 00:01:14,414
The T-64 was a revolution: a flat, compact
hull, an autoloader and a three-man crew.

14
00:01:14,414 --> 00:01:20,791
Ukraine inherited most of them, and Moscow
now fields survivors captured in Donbas

15
00:01:20,791 --> 00:01:25,379
since twenty-fourteen. The same factory,
on the other side.

16
00:01:25,379 --> 00:01:31,629
The T-72B3 is the workhorse. Sosna-U
thermal sight, Kontakt-5 explosive armor,

17
00:01:31,629 --> 00:01:36,037
a modernized
hundred-and-twenty-five-millimeter gun. It

18
00:01:36,037 --> 00:01:42,367
is not the newest tank Russia has. It is
the one there are most of, and the one

19
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:44,290
Ukraine sees most often.

20
00:01:44,290 --> 00:01:50,226
The T-80 runs on a gas turbine, and it
drinks fuel like an aircraft. The BVM

21
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:56,631
upgrade gives it Relikt armor and a modern
fire-control system. Fast, cold-blooded

22
00:01:56,631 --> 00:02:01,551
and expensive: the pride of a force that
cannot always fuel it.

23
00:02:01,551 --> 00:02:06,690
The T-90 introduced Shtora-one: two
infrared dazzlers that try to blind

24
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:12,624
incoming missiles. In practice the system
has not saved many crews, but the tank's

25
00:02:12,624 --> 00:02:16,677
welded turret and
hundred-and-twenty-five-millimeter gun

26
00:02:16,677 --> 00:02:18,124
keep it in the line.

27
00:02:18,124 --> 00:02:23,587
The BMPT Terminator was built for a
problem tanks keep meeting in cities:

28
00:02:23,587 --> 00:02:29,200
infantry with anti-tank missiles. Two
thirty-millimeter cannons, four Ataka

29
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:35,037
missiles, and a hull that is still a T-72.
Few were made, fewer are committed.

30
00:02:35,037 --> 00:02:40,876
The T-90M Proryv is Russia's most capable
tank in service: a new welded turret,

31
00:02:40,876 --> 00:02:46,272
Kalina fire control, Relikt armor and
better situational awareness. It is

32
00:02:46,272 --> 00:02:51,594
genuinely modern, which is exactly why so
few are risked near the front.

33
00:02:51,594 --> 00:02:57,970
Next, the wheeled vehicles: light armor
for escort, patrol and internal security.

34
00:02:57,970 --> 00:03:03,751
The Tigr is the ubiquitous four-by-four of
the Russian army: light, fast and

35
00:03:03,751 --> 00:03:09,913
thin-skinned. A remote machine-gun mount,
ten passengers, and enough speed to run

36
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:11,967
from a fight it cannot win.

37
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:16,048
The KamAZ Patrol is a National Guard
vehicle: a V-hull, a

38
00:03:16,048 --> 00:03:22,062
twelve-point-seven-millimeter mount, and a
role that is mostly internal security and

39
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:25,570
rear-area escort rather than the line of
contact.

40
00:03:25,570 --> 00:03:31,118
Typhoon-K is a mine-resistant truck:
twenty tonnes, a V-shaped belly, and an

41
00:03:31,118 --> 00:03:37,250
armored capsule for the crew. It survives
blasts that would destroy a BTR. But it is

42
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:39,586
a truck, not a fighting vehicle.

43
00:03:39,586 --> 00:03:44,479
The airborne Typhoon is smaller and
lighter so that it can be flown and

44
00:03:44,479 --> 00:03:50,268
dropped. Same protection philosophy, half
the weight, and a machine gun on the roof.

45
00:03:50,268 --> 00:03:55,250
The Z-STS Akhmat appeared in
twenty-twenty-two, built for Chechen

46
00:03:55,250 --> 00:04:01,075
units. Ten tonnes, a machine gun and a
grenade launcher, and a hull designed

47
00:04:01,075 --> 00:04:04,523
around ambushes and mines on secondary
roads.

48
00:04:04,523 --> 00:04:09,715
Astais is the newest member of the family,
a Remdiesel design shown in

49
00:04:09,715 --> 00:04:15,278
twenty-twenty-three. Modular armor, a
remote weapon station, and production

50
00:04:15,278 --> 00:04:18,913
numbers measured in dozens rather than
thousands.

51
00:04:18,913 --> 00:04:23,147
Tank destroyers: machines built to ambush
armor from cover.

52
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:28,016
Shturm-S carries radio-guided Shturm
missiles on an MT-LB chassis, hidden

53
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:33,219
behind a retractable launcher. It shoots
from cover, then moves: a tank killer

54
00:04:33,219 --> 00:04:35,154
built to ambush, not to duel.

55
00:04:35,154 --> 00:04:41,448
The Sprut-SD is a light tank in all but
name: a hundred-and-twenty-five-millimeter

56
00:04:41,448 --> 00:04:47,283
gun on an airborne chassis. It can be
dropped by parachute, and it can swim.

57
00:04:47,283 --> 00:04:50,430
That is a lot of gun for eighteen tonnes.

58
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:55,990
Krizantema-S guides two supersonic
missiles at once, by radar beam or laser,

59
00:04:55,990 --> 00:05:02,209
day or night. One crew, two targets, and a
chassis borrowed from the BMP-3. It is the

60
00:05:02,209 --> 00:05:05,940
most sophisticated anti-tank vehicle
Russia fields.

61
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:10,637
Konkurs-M is older, cheaper and
everywhere: a tandem warhead on a

62
00:05:10,637 --> 00:05:16,562
wire-guided missile, mounted on vehicles
or fired from the ground. Simple, proven,

63
00:05:16,562 --> 00:05:20,174
and produced in numbers no Western system
matches.

64
00:05:20,174 --> 00:05:23,411
Reconnaissance: the vehicles that go
first.

65
00:05:23,411 --> 00:05:29,083
The BRDM-2 has been scouting since
nineteen-sixty-two: four wheels, two more

66
00:05:29,083 --> 00:05:34,531
in the middle, a small turret and the
ability to swim. It is a scout that

67
00:05:34,531 --> 00:05:37,367
survives by being small and unnoticed.

68
00:05:37,367 --> 00:05:43,597
BRM-1K is the battlefield reconnaissance
version of the BMP-1: a thirty-millimeter

69
00:05:43,597 --> 00:05:49,903
cannon, ground-surveillance radar, and a
crew of six who are trained to look rather

70
00:05:49,903 --> 00:05:50,967
than to fight.

71
00:05:50,967 --> 00:05:56,353
The Vystrel is a KamAZ truck turned
armored car, used mostly by border guards:

72
00:05:56,353 --> 00:06:02,155
a fourteen-point-five-millimeter turret, a
rear ramp, and a long service life in the

73
00:06:02,155 --> 00:06:02,776
Caucasus.

74
00:06:02,776 --> 00:06:09,535
Armored personnel carriers: the vehicles
that carry infantry, and the ones their

75
00:06:09,535 --> 00:06:11,056
crews trust least.

76
00:06:11,056 --> 00:06:16,021
The BTR-50 belongs in a museum: a
nineteen-fifties tracked amphibious

77
00:06:16,021 --> 00:06:21,849
carrier. It came back into service from
storage, thinly armored and slow, because

78
00:06:21,849 --> 00:06:25,302
Russia needed hulls more than it needed
quality.

79
00:06:25,302 --> 00:06:31,331
The MT-LB is the mule of the Russian army.
Artillery tractor, ambulance, mortar

80
00:06:31,331 --> 00:06:37,283
carrier, and in this war a platform for
improvised armor, naval guns and drone

81
00:06:37,283 --> 00:06:40,870
cages. Seventy years old, and still
everywhere.

82
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:46,728
The BTR-70 uses two petrol engines and
eight wheels, with side doors so narrow

83
00:06:46,728 --> 00:06:52,886
they are dangerous under fire. Its crews
prefer the later BTR-80, for reasons that

84
00:06:52,886 --> 00:06:54,613
are easy to understand.

85
00:06:54,613 --> 00:07:00,475
The BTR-D is the airborne carrier: light,
tracked, and designed to be dropped. It

86
00:07:00,475 --> 00:07:06,627
has almost no protection, which is why its
crews ride on the roof rather than inside.

87
00:07:06,627 --> 00:07:11,477
The BTR-80 fixed the BTR-70's worst
problem with side hatches and a diesel

88
00:07:11,477 --> 00:07:15,541
engine, and kept the
fourteen-point-five-millimeter turret. It

89
00:07:15,541 --> 00:07:19,932
is the standard wheeled carrier, and
Ukraine has captured hundreds.

90
00:07:19,932 --> 00:07:25,186
The BMO-T is a heavy carrier built on a
T-72 hull to move flamethrower teams:

91
00:07:25,186 --> 00:07:30,441
forty tonnes of armor, no main gun, and a
job that puts it close to infantry,

92
00:07:30,441 --> 00:07:32,829
exactly where anti-tank teams wait.

93
00:07:32,829 --> 00:07:38,322
Rakushka means little shell. It is a
paratrooper carrier with thirteen seats, a

94
00:07:38,322 --> 00:07:44,162
machine gun, and enough buoyancy to swim.
Light, roomy, and only as safe as its thin

95
00:07:44,162 --> 00:07:45,205
aluminum sides.

96
00:07:45,205 --> 00:07:50,612
Infantry fighting vehicles: designed to
fight alongside the men they carry.

97
00:07:50,612 --> 00:07:56,820
The BMP-1 invented the infantry fighting
vehicle: a seventy-three-millimeter gun, a

98
00:07:56,820 --> 00:08:02,655
missile on the barrel, and eight soldiers
inside. Its armor stops bullets, and

99
00:08:02,655 --> 00:08:03,552
little else.

100
00:08:03,552 --> 00:08:08,867
The BMP-2 replaced the low-pressure gun
with a thirty-millimeter cannon that

101
00:08:08,867 --> 00:08:14,532
elevates high enough to hit aircraft and
rooftops. It is the most common infantry

102
00:08:14,532 --> 00:08:16,420
vehicle in Russian service.

103
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:22,367
The BMD-2 is the airborne version of the
BMP-2: eight tonnes, a thirty-millimeter

104
00:08:22,367 --> 00:08:27,874
cannon, and armor you can hear. It is
built to be dropped, not to survive a

105
00:08:27,874 --> 00:08:28,682
direct hit.

106
00:08:28,682 --> 00:08:33,188
The BMP-3 is the most heavily armed
infantry vehicle in the world: a

107
00:08:33,188 --> 00:08:38,555
hundred-millimeter gun that also fires
missiles, plus a thirty-millimeter cannon.

108
00:08:38,555 --> 00:08:43,458
In this war it is often used as light
artillery, firing from behind cover.

109
00:08:43,458 --> 00:08:48,323
The BMD-4M carries the same
hundred-millimeter gun as the BMP-3 on an

110
00:08:48,323 --> 00:08:53,962
airborne hull, with add-on anti-drone
armor and a modern sight. Thirteen tonnes,

111
00:08:53,962 --> 00:08:56,782
and a crew that knows speed beats armor.

112
00:08:56,782 --> 00:09:02,773
Self-propelled artillery: the arm doing
most of the damage in this war.

113
00:09:02,773 --> 00:09:07,286
Akatsiya has been the standard Soviet
self-propelled howitzer since

114
00:09:07,286 --> 00:09:11,665
nineteen-seventy-one: a
hundred-and-fifty-two-millimeter gun on a

115
00:09:11,665 --> 00:09:16,852
tracked chassis. Old, mechanical, and
still doing most of the killing in this

116
00:09:16,852 --> 00:09:17,121
war.

117
00:09:17,121 --> 00:09:22,761
Gvozdika is small, light and amphibious,
with a hundred-and-twenty-two-millimeter

118
00:09:22,761 --> 00:09:28,122
gun. It follows infantry closely and
shoots fast, and it is the most numerous

119
00:09:28,122 --> 00:09:30,211
self-propelled gun Russia has.

120
00:09:30,211 --> 00:09:34,626
Tyulpan fires the largest mortar ever
fielded: two hundred and forty

121
00:09:34,626 --> 00:09:40,079
millimeters, loaded from the breech, with
a shell that can flatten a building. Fewer

122
00:09:40,079 --> 00:09:43,520
than a hundred exist, and each one is a
siege weapon.

123
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:48,513
Pion is the heaviest self-propelled gun in
service: two hundred and three

124
00:09:48,513 --> 00:09:54,054
millimeters, forty-six tonnes, and a range
beyond thirty-seven kilometers. It was

125
00:09:54,054 --> 00:09:59,457
built to fire nuclear shells, and it fires
far more often than anyone expected.

126
00:09:59,457 --> 00:10:03,336
Giatsint-S has a long
hundred-and-fifty-two-millimeter barrel

127
00:10:03,336 --> 00:10:07,915
with exceptional range and brutal recoil.
It is accurate, expensive, and

128
00:10:07,915 --> 00:10:09,823
increasingly rare on the line.

129
00:10:09,823 --> 00:10:15,945
Nona is a gun, a mortar and a howitzer in
one, mounted on an airborne chassis that

130
00:10:15,945 --> 00:10:21,842
swims. A hundred and twenty millimeters,
high elevation, and the ability to hit

131
00:10:21,842 --> 00:10:24,977
anything from a trench line to a hillside.

132
00:10:24,977 --> 00:10:30,199
Solntsepyok is not artillery. It is a
thermobaric launcher on a T-72 chassis:

133
00:10:30,199 --> 00:10:35,623
thirty rockets, a wall of pressure and
heat, and a range so short that it has to

134
00:10:35,623 --> 00:10:37,183
come close to use them.

135
00:10:37,183 --> 00:10:42,925
Msta-S is the modern tracked howitzer: a
hundred-and-fifty-two-millimeter gun, an

136
00:10:42,925 --> 00:10:47,959
autoloader, and a computerised
fire-control system. It shoots and moves

137
00:10:47,959 --> 00:10:52,213
in minutes, which is the only way to
survive drone spotting.

138
00:10:52,213 --> 00:10:56,286
Nona-SVK puts the
hundred-and-twenty-millimeter gun-mortar

139
00:10:56,286 --> 00:11:02,115
on an eight-wheeled BTR-80 hull. Faster on
roads than the tracked version, and used

140
00:11:02,115 --> 00:11:04,151
to support infantry directly.

141
00:11:04,151 --> 00:11:09,616
Khosta is a Gvozdika rebuilt around a
hundred-and-twenty-millimeter gun-mortar,

142
00:11:09,616 --> 00:11:15,288
giving one chassis the jobs of two. Few
were converted, and most stay close to the

143
00:11:15,288 --> 00:11:16,810
infantry they support.

144
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:20,793
Floks is new: a
hundred-and-twenty-millimeter gun-mortar

145
00:11:20,793 --> 00:11:26,697
on a wheeled Ural chassis, first seen in
twenty-twenty-three. Cheaper and faster to

146
00:11:26,697 --> 00:11:30,609
build than a tracked gun, and now in
serial production.

147
00:11:30,609 --> 00:11:34,200
Malva is the wheeled answer to drone
artillery: a

148
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:40,283
hundred-and-fifty-two-millimeter gun on an
eight-wheel-drive chassis that can shoot

149
00:11:40,283 --> 00:11:44,901
and leave in under two minutes. Few exist,
but more are coming.

150
00:11:44,901 --> 00:11:50,214
Engineering vehicles: the unglamorous
machines that make movement possible.

151
00:11:50,214 --> 00:11:56,079
MTU-72 lays a twenty-meter bridge from a
T-72 hull in about three minutes. Armor

152
00:11:56,079 --> 00:12:02,164
keeps the crew alive while it works, which
is the entire point of an armored bridge

153
00:12:02,164 --> 00:12:02,603
layer.

154
00:12:02,603 --> 00:12:08,093
UR-77 Meteorit clears minefields with a
rocket-towed hose of explosive: two

155
00:12:08,093 --> 00:12:14,095
hundred meters of lane, opened in seconds,
by a vehicle that has to drive into the

156
00:12:14,095 --> 00:12:15,486
minefield to do it.

157
00:12:15,486 --> 00:12:21,067
BAT-2 is a bulldozer that can keep up with
tanks: a blade, a crane and a winch on a

158
00:12:21,067 --> 00:12:26,044
tracked chassis, used to cut routes
through forests and rubble under fire.

159
00:12:26,044 --> 00:12:32,274
IMR-2 is the combat engineer's tank: a
telescopic crane arm, a dozer blade, and a

160
00:12:32,274 --> 00:12:38,658
T-72 hull to survive the work. It clears
obstacles while people are shooting at it.

161
00:12:38,658 --> 00:12:44,330
GMZ-3 is a minelayer: it ploughs and
buries anti-tank mines at speed, turning

162
00:12:44,330 --> 00:12:50,223
an open field into an obstacle belt in
minutes. The Russian army still lays more

163
00:12:50,223 --> 00:12:51,770
mines than any other.

164
00:12:51,770 --> 00:12:57,861
BREM-1 is the recovery vehicle built on a
T-72: a crane, a winch, and a crew willing

165
00:12:57,861 --> 00:13:03,735
to drive toward broken tanks. Every hull
it recovers is one that does not have to

166
00:13:03,735 --> 00:13:04,605
be replaced.

167
00:13:04,605 --> 00:13:09,644
UBIM is the newest engineer vehicle:
recovery, breaching and clearing on one

168
00:13:09,644 --> 00:13:14,816
armored eight-wheeled chassis, with a
manipulator arm and a dozer blade. Fewer

169
00:13:14,816 --> 00:13:16,672
than a dozen have been seen.

170
00:13:16,672 --> 00:13:22,632
Fifty-two vehicles. A force rebuilding in
public, losing armor faster than it can be

171
00:13:22,632 --> 00:13:28,237
replaced, and adapting faster than most
expected. Specifications here come from

172
00:13:28,237 --> 00:13:34,126
open sources, and recorded losses are only
the ones visually confirmed, which means

173
00:13:34,126 --> 00:13:39,660
the real numbers are higher. If you want
more breakdowns like this, subscribe.

174
00:13:41,860 --> 00:13:47,686
Every vehicle here is confirmed in Russian
service in 2026. One representative

175
00:13:47,686 --> 00:13:53,511
variant per family; sub-variants are
grouped. Specifications are compiled from

176
00:13:53,511 --> 00:13:59,188
open sources and are approximate. Nothing
in this film comes from classified

177
00:13:59,188 --> 00:13:59,860
material.

178
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:14,485
Oryx, February 2026: T-72 family 1,815
confirmed losses. BMP-2 over 2,000. BTR

179
00:14:14,485 --> 00:14:17,860
series over 1,000.

180
00:14:17,860 --> 00:14:23,525
Entered service: the year the type was
adopted, not the first prototype. Combat

181
00:14:23,525 --> 00:14:29,119
weight: loaded, as fielded, not empty.
Crew: the vehicle's own crew; dismounts

182
00:14:29,119 --> 00:14:34,497
are counted separately. Loss figures
circulating online count only visually

183
00:14:34,497 --> 00:14:35,860
confirmed vehicles.

184
00:14:35,860 --> 00:14:41,673
Open sources: manufacturer data, ministry
releases, reference works. Figures differ

185
00:14:41,673 --> 00:14:46,856
between sources; numbers here are
mid-range estimates. Production and loss

186
00:14:46,856 --> 00:14:52,389
totals are published by neither side.
These are inventories, not assessments of

187
00:14:52,389 --> 00:14:53,860
combat effectiveness.
